## Other Notable Crusades

**People’s Crusade (1096)**
Preceded First Crusade; peasant army led by Peter the Hermit; destroyed in Anatolia

**Crusade of 1101**
Follow-up to First Crusade; three armies destroyed in Anatolia

**Albigensian Crusade (1209–1229)**
France, Papacy vs. Cathars in southern France (Languedoc)

**Children’s Crusade (1212)**
Disorganized movement of young people; ended in dispersal, death, or enslavement

**Northern/Baltic Crusades (1147–1410)**
Teutonic Knights, Livonian Order, Denmark, Sweden vs. pagan Wends, Prussians, Lithuanians, Estonians
Places: Baltic region, Prussia, Livonia, Finland

**Reconquista-related Crusades (various, 1000s–1492)**
Spanish and Portuguese kingdoms vs. Moorish taifas and Almohads
Places: Iberian Peninsula

**Alexandrian Crusade (1365)**
Cyprus (Peter I) vs. Mamluks; brief sack of Alexandria

**Crusade of Nicopolis (1396)**
Hungary, France, Burgundy, others vs. Ottoman Empire
Place: Nicopolis (Bulgaria)
Outcome: Ottoman victory

**Crusade of Varna (1443–1444)**
Hungary, Poland, others vs. Ottoman Empire
Place: Varna (Bulgaria)
Outcome: Ottoman victory; Hungarian king killed​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​


-- politics ---

This period saw dramatic political transformations across the region. Here are the major shifts:

## The Rise and Fall of Empires

The **Mongol Empire** (1206–1368) was the defining political force of the 13th century. Genghis Khan and his successors created the largest contiguous land empire in history, stretching from Korea to Poland. Its fragmentation into successor states—the Golden Horde, Ilkhanate, Chagatai Khanate—reshaped Central Asia and Russia for centuries. The **Timurid Empire** rose in its wake under Tamerlane in the late 1300s, devastating much of Persia and the Middle East.

The **Byzantine Empire** entered terminal decline. Already weakened, it was shattered by the Fourth Crusade (1204), when Crusaders sacked Constantinople and established Latin states on its territory. Though partially restored in 1261, it never recovered, and **Constantinople fell to the Ottoman Turks in 1453**—a symbolic end to the medieval era.

## Western Europe’s Transformation

**Feudalism began breaking down** after the Black Death. Labor shortages gave peasants leverage, leading to uprisings like the English Peasants’ Revolt (1381) and the German Bundschuh movements. Serfdom declined in Western Europe while intensifying in the East.

**Centralized monarchies emerged** in France, England, and Spain. The Hundred Years’ War (1337–1453) between England and France, despite its devastation, ultimately strengthened French royal power. The **Reconquista** culminated in 1492 when Spain unified under Ferdinand and Isabella.

## Eastern Europe and Russia

The **Golden Horde’s grip on Russia weakened** through the 14th–15th centuries. Moscow gradually emerged as the dominant Russian principality, formally throwing off Mongol rule in 1480. The **Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth** became one of Europe’s largest states.

## Religious and Institutional Power

The **Papacy’s authority peaked then fractured**. The Avignon Papacy (1309–1377) and the Great Schism (1378–1417)—when rival popes competed for legitimacy—severely damaged Church prestige, setting the stage for later Reformation challenges.​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​



--- weather and plague ---

This period saw some of the most dramatic external shocks in recorded history for this region. Here’s an overview:

## Climate Shifts

The era began during the **Medieval Warm Period** (roughly 900–1300), which allowed agricultural expansion, population growth, and Viking colonization of Greenland. This was followed by the onset of the **Little Ice Age** (beginning around 1300–1350), which brought harsher winters, shorter growing seasons, and advancing glaciers. While not a true ice age, it significantly stressed agricultural systems.

## Famines

The **Great Famine of 1315–1317** struck Northern Europe with devastating force. Unusually cold, wet weather caused widespread crop failures for several consecutive years. Estimates suggest 10–25% of the population in affected areas died. It marked the end of the medieval population boom and left populations weakened for what came next.

## The Black Death

The **Black Death (1346–1353)** was the single most catastrophic event of this period—possibly in all of human history. Originating in Central Asia, it traveled along Silk Road trade routes and arrived in Europe via Genoese ships from Crimea. It killed an estimated 30–60% of Europe’s population (perhaps 25–50 million people) and similarly devastated the Middle East and parts of Asia. Recurring plague outbreaks continued throughout the 14th and 15th centuries.

## Mongol Invasions

The **Mongol conquests (1206–1300s)** reshaped Central Asia, Persia, and Eastern Europe. Entire cities were razed, irrigation systems destroyed, and populations massacred or displaced. Some historians estimate tens of millions died. The destruction of Central Asian agricultural infrastructure had effects lasting centuries.

## Combined Effects

These pressures were interconnected: climate stress weakened populations, famine reduced resistance to disease, and Mongol trade networks inadvertently spread the plague. By 1400, Europe’s population may have been half what it was in 1300. This demographic collapse transformed labor relations, land use, and ultimately helped trigger major social and economic changes.​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​